question_answers.global-heldout-003:
Papers generally retain mRMR’s core trade-off—maximize relevance to the target while minimizing redundancy among selected features—but adapt how relevance and redundancy are evaluated for the data domain [1][5][7].

For time-indexed data, they often:

- avoid flattening each time series into a single feature vector, since flattening can lose temporal-order information [1][8];
- evaluate multivariate trajectories directly, representing each individual as measurements across variables and time steps [1];
- replace the standard featurewise mutual-information calculations with temporal or trajectory-aware comparisons, including dynamic time warping (DTW), which is reported to improve robustness for temporal data [5][7].

More broadly, mRMR variants may also replace mutual information with domain-appropriate statistics—for example, an F-statistic—to improve efficiency or suit the application [6]. Thus, the usual adaptation is not to discard the relevance–redundancy principle, but to change the similarity or association measure and the evaluation procedure so they respect temporal structure or other domain-specific properties [1][5][6][7].
